#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6694ff0b-ce44-5267-940f-03bc7eaa739d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338537} {:answer/id #uuid "04614be6-49ef-5da8-8cb7-8d07bfe2629f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338538} {:answer/id #uuid "5eecea38-b79a-5b54-9dd2-2616c15784b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338539} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cd3210-ff40-58f5-a265-8951fd4a2f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338540} {:answer/id #uuid "f472db3d-2365-5ac8-aabd-622d96e4a11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f472db3d-2365-5ac8-aabd-622d96e4a11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338541} :answer-field/id #uuid "d260d722-a004-5ffb-8989-3c47ec75d87d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb71e2f3-58c2-5fe5-b7fd-919a06f927d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105573" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/3088d088689bbd0dfd33abe8240c6f02cceb257937fc1fccb8ba3df151dbfaab.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7589e4d-9501-5cb7-a8be-6a05142e9723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338544} {:answer/id #uuid "93b3d037-d808-54ef-a3a5-b7d76867a9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338545} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1f6cee-c0e4-5688-91d5-b3fd28fbb35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338546} {:answer/id #uuid "2e511b20-f647-5935-b693-8f0e6e7ff1f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338547} {:answer/id #uuid "a173872e-2039-5f10-be0a-23d2a52c4a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7589e4d-9501-5cb7-a8be-6a05142e9723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338544} :answer-field/id #uuid "664359e7-76a5-5834-98f5-947262b90783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a9b7bd5-4597-5a75-b03e-038a1e45c570" :question/math-academy-id "q-105576" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/cf932f4f02a216d5111da0748cb04f815a7d2e85af5db2f9282f5aefc359b816.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=0$\n- $∇\\cdot F>0$\n- $∇\\cdot F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec0cfae-efb5-578f-9a25-ccb00c9e91fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338551} {:answer/id #uuid "30613d7b-1211-51e8-a015-8b7f4b5597e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338552} {:answer/id #uuid "63efa5e9-7250-5e71-9cbd-b4667d2d7ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338553} {:answer/id #uuid "a076a420-51a8-57e9-bea4-675a98b5ff2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338554} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5073d6-9aac-5387-ac87-532b3b27db45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63efa5e9-7250-5e71-9cbd-b4667d2d7ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338553} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fd2ca8-4514-54e8-917a-8e0bba17222f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa5cdcb7-c1f3-500e-aa56-d1ffbcb0b240" :question/math-academy-id "q-123200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x-y)^{3}\\,i+(y+z)^{3}\\,j+(x+z)^{3}\\,k.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+z)^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x+z)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-y)^{2}+3(y+z)^{2}+3(x+z)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,2,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(-2,2,2) & =3((-2)-(2))^{2}+3((2)+(2))^{2}+3((-2)+(2))^{2} \\\\  & =3(-4)^{2}+3(4)^{2}+3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f47e201-105a-579a-8cde-fcb6bf8cfa2a" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Divergence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ad2e67-a50c-596a-8987-67f6f8d1c7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338558} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bb5d06-a6a3-5460-9aa5-02bfdb993f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338559} {:answer/id #uuid "9784d2fc-9a09-5586-98e9-8eee5ac0ee6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338560} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf5550f-33c6-5bdb-bbd9-cd1571f04de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338561} {:answer/id #uuid "9125efa4-484c-51e4-a471-b8f823ef7c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ad2e67-a50c-596a-8987-67f6f8d1c7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338558} :answer-field/id #uuid "4374a794-c5c6-53a0-a9b2-53769b244d79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f93fb39f-f5ac-593f-a746-5f36aa40a31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131193" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/243496443c3878c90fb67888add6c6ad6d3e69aa20ba24f42ee89d58a2742534.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060047/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846328ba-9f46-504b-9d15-7aac7dcde82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132207} {:answer/id #uuid "93d6d102-de60-5f60-bccb-fa540960a2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132208} {:answer/id #uuid "589e6c00-53c4-5663-9ce0-decf33177e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132209} {:answer/id #uuid "65d02e21-77c2-5434-b242-81efe4abbef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132210} {:answer/id #uuid "6949b4c5-46b5-5c15-ba5e-fefd5c24b3be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846328ba-9f46-504b-9d15-7aac7dcde82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132207} :answer-field/id #uuid "398f85a0-f775-594e-8cc9-b150f453028f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17dfa481-85bd-5863-b436-260f29cb7515" :question/math-academy-id "q-41839" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
